  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes major challenges and mitigation strategies for metal-anode batteries and separators in non-aqueous versus aqueous systems. Across the top, both anode types suffer from dendrite formation, but the failure modes differ. In non-aqueous electrolytes, the main issue is dendrite growth on the anode surface, with suggested fixes centered on stabilizing the interphase, using artificial protective layers, alloying, and designing 3D anodes. In aqueous systems, the anode also shows corrosion and hydrogen evolution, indicating added parasitic reactions beyond dendrites; proposed solutions focus on suppressing the hydrogen reaction, optimizing electric fields, alloying, and 3D structures.\n\nThe bottom half shifts to separators. In non-aqueous cells, separators appear vulnerable to dendrite penetration, formation of dead metal, crossover of radicals or redox mediators, and mechanical deformation caused by electrode volume changes. The remedies emphasize stronger, more selective, and flexible separators. In aqueous cells, ion-exchange membranes such as PEM and BPM are shown with problems including product crossover, degradation, poor pH buffering, high resistance, and sluggish water splitting. Suggested approaches include surface modification, catalysts to accelerate water splitting, and membrane-free designs. Overall, the figure contrasts how water introduces extra chemical and transport challenges throughout the cell.",
